126 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 42 0 R
/E (The design implements a 4-bit up/down counter using an always_ff block with an active-low asynchronous reset. The sensitivity list includes both posedge clk and negedge rst_n, allowing the counter to reset immediately whenever rst_n is asserted low. When reset is inactive, the counter increments by one on each rising clock edge if up_down is high, and decrements by one if up_down is low. Since the counter is 4 bits wide, it naturally wraps from 15 to 0 while counting up and from 0 to 15 while counting down.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
127 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 42 0 R
/E (The testbench generates a periodic clock and applies different input stimuli to verify the counter's functionality. It first allows the counter to count for a few clock cycles, then asserts the asynchronous reset between clock edges to confirm that the counter clears immediately. After releasing the reset, the testbench verifies the counting-up sequence, followed by the counting-down sequence, while printing the counter value at each clock edge. This ensures that all required behaviors, including wraparound, are tested.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
128 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 42 0 R
/E (The terminal output displays the counter value throughout the simulation. It shows the counter incrementing before reset, immediately clearing to 0 when the asynchronous reset is asserted, then resuming normal operation after reset is released. The output also confirms correct incrementing, decrementing, and wraparound between 15 and 0, demonstrating that the design behaves as expected.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
129 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 42 0 R
/E (The waveform confirms the sequential operation of the counter. The count output changes only on the rising edge of the clock during normal operation, while the asynchronous reset forces the counter to 0000 immediately when rst_n goes low, regardless of the clock. After the reset is deasserted, counting resumes on subsequent clock edges. The waveform also clearly shows both the count-up and count-down sequences, including the expected wraparound from 1111 to 0000 and from 0000 to 1111.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
