225 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 45 0 R
/E (The design implements a two-flip-flop reset synchronizer that converts an asynchronous active-low reset into a synchronized reset signal. Both flip-flops use an asynchronous active-low reset, allowing the reset to be asserted immediately regardless of the clock. When rst_n_async is deasserted, the first flip-flop captures a logic high on the next rising clock edge, and the second flip-flop captures it on the following clock edge. As a result, the synchronized reset \(rst_n_sync\) is released only after two clock cycles, ensuring that reset deassertion occurs safely on a clock edge and reducing the risk of metastability.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
226 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 45 0 R
/E (The testbench generates a periodic clock and applies several reset scenarios to verify the synchronizer's behavior. It first asserts the asynchronous reset between clock edges, then releases it to observe the synchronized deassertion. Additional tests assert the reset at or near a clock edge and apply a short reset pulse to ensure consistent operation under different timing conditions. A waveform dump and signal monitor are included to observe the behavior of the reset signals throughout the simulation.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
227 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 45 0 R
/E (The terminal output displays the values of clk, rst_n_async, and rst_n_sync during the simulation. It confirms that whenever the asynchronous reset is asserted, the synchronized reset immediately becomes active. After the asynchronous reset is released, the output remains in the reset state until two rising clock edges have occurred, demonstrating the intended synchronization delay. The printed values verify that the synchronizer behaves correctly under all test cases.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
228 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 45 0 R
/E (The waveform illustrates the operation of the reset synchronizer. When rst_n_async is asserted low, both internal flip-flops \(ff1 and ff2\) and the synchronized reset \(rst_n_sync\) are cleared immediately without waiting for a clock edge. When the asynchronous reset is deasserted, ff1 changes state on the first rising edge of the clock, while ff2 changes on the following rising edge. Consequently, rst_n_sync is released only after two clock cycles and always transitions on a clock edge, preventing recovery-time violations and ensuring a stable, synchronized reset signal for the rest of the circuit.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
